        <p>Ketamine nasal spray has moved from clinical anesthesia suites into research labs, therapeutic practices, and sophisticated consumer markets. Unlike intramuscular injection or oral consumption, intranasal delivery offers a unique pharmacokinetic profile — faster onset than oral, slower peak than IV, and a duration that sits comfortably in the middle. But not all ketamine nasal sprays are created equal. The isomer ratio, concentration per spray, flavor matrix, and even the pH of the solution all determine the quality of the experience.</p>

        <p>This guide breaks down everything you need to know about ketamine nasal sprays: how to dose them properly, what effects to expect at each tier, how S-isomer and R-isomer formulations differ, and why flavor profiles are not just a gimmick — they are a functional part of the formulation.</p>

        <h2>S-Isomer vs R-Isomer: The Molecular Divide</h2>

        <p>Ketamine is a chiral molecule, meaning it exists as two mirror-image enantiomers: <strong>S-ketamine (esketamine)</strong> and <strong>R-ketamine (arketamine)</strong>. Your body processes these two isomers differently. S-ketamine binds roughly three to four times more potently to the NMDA receptor than R-ketamine, which means lower doses produce equivalent dissociative effects. R-ketamine, meanwhile, tends to produce a longer-lasting, more physically sedating experience with less cognitive distortion.</p>

        <p>The FDA-approved nasal spray <a href="https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-new-nasal-spray-medication-treatment-resistant-depression-available-only-certified" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spravato</a> uses exclusively S-ketamine for this reason — it achieves therapeutic antidepressant effects at doses that would be sub-perceptual with a racemic mixture. Understanding this distinction is critical before you choose a product.</p>

