        <h2>Storage and Shelf Life</h2>

        <p>Ketamine nasal spray is more sensitive to degradation than most people assume. The molecule is photolytic — it breaks down under UV light — and hydrolytic, meaning it slowly degrades in aqueous solution over time. Proper storage is not optional if you want consistent potency.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Store at 4–25°C (39–77°F). Refrigeration extends shelf life but is not required for short-term storage. Never freeze — ice crystals can rupture the active ingredient matrix and alter spray consistency.</li>
            <li><strong>Light:</strong> Keep in the original amber or opaque bottle. UV exposure degrades ketamine into norketamine and dehydronorketamine, both of which have different pharmacological profiles.</li>
            <li><strong>Position:</strong> Store upright. The dip tube must remain submerged in the solution. If the tube dries out, the first few sprays will be air bubbles delivering inconsistent doses.</li>
            <li><strong>Shelf life:</strong> Unopened, 24 months from manufacture. Opened, use within 90 days. After 90 days, potency loss of 5–10% is typical due to oxidation.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Safety Protocols: Non-Negotiable Rules</h2>

        <p>Ketamine is one of the safest anesthetics in controlled clinical settings. In uncontrolled environments, the risk profile changes. These rules are not suggestions — they are the baseline for responsible use.</p>

        <!-- VISUAL: SAFETY TIPS -->
        <div class="safety-grid reveal">
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-warn">✕</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Never Combine with Alcohol</strong>Both substances depress the central nervous system. The combination dramatically increases the risk of respiratory depression, aspiration, and unconsciousness. This is the most common cause of ketamine-related emergency room visits.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-ok">✓</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Use a Sitter for High Doses</strong>A sober, trusted person should be present for any session exceeding 6 sprays. Their only job is to ensure you remain in a safe position (on your side, not your back) and to call for help if breathing becomes irregular.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-warn">✕</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Avoid if You Have Hypertension</strong>Ketamine causes a transient rise in blood pressure and heart rate. If you have uncontrolled hypertension, cardiovascular disease, or a history of stroke, intranasal ketamine is contraindicated.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-ok">✓</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Wait 14 Days Between Sessions</strong>Chronic daily use leads to bladder toxicity (ketamine-induced cystitis) and cognitive decline. The 14-day rule preserves the magic, protects your bladder, and prevents tolerance escalation.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-warn">✕</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Do Not Drive Within 8 Hours</strong>Even if you feel "fine," reaction time and depth perception remain impaired for hours after the subjective effects fade. Plan your transportation before you dose.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="safety-card">
                <div class="safety-icon safety-ok">✓</div>
                <div class="safety-text"><strong>Test Your Product</strong>Always verify purity with a reagent test or by requesting the batch COA. Counterfeit ketamine sprays containing 2-FDCK, DCK, or other analogs are increasingly common and have different safety profiles.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

        <p>Ketamine nasal spray represents one of the most accessible and controllable routes of administration for this versatile compound. The key to a safe, meaningful experience lies in three variables: <strong>isomer selection</strong> (S for intensity, R for duration), <strong>conservative dosing</strong> (start low, titrate slow), and <strong>environmental preparation</strong> (set, setting, and a sitter).</p>

